Sometimes accountability arrives because people refuse to accept an unfair arrangement any longer.

And sometimes the people challenging a system become the catalyst for its reform.

But being the consequence doesn’t have to mean becoming the threat. It can mean building something better, exposing contradictions, competing successfully, or changing the rules through collective action.

The real measure of power isn’t how much you can destroy. It’s how much you can change without sacrificing what you value.

Be the consequence of your convictions, not the casualty of your anger.